[Unit]
Description=Run tirra-collect.service at weekends (Sat/Sun 18:00 UTC) — the weekend half of 7-day collection

[Timer]
# WEEKEND HALF OF A 7-DAY SPLIT. tirra-collect.timer / tirra-chain.timer run
# Mon..Fri 18:00 UTC, which is the right cadence for the market-hours sources
# that dominate daily_collection — CFTC (COT, Friday release), FINRA short
# volume, instrument_universe closes, options_chain. None of them publish at
# the weekend.
#
# It is the wrong cadence for the *continuous, time-gated* sources sitting in
# the same DAG: ais_vessel, whale_alert, dns_monitor, cert_transparency,
# finra_short_volume, form144, political_risk, academic_preprints,
# regulatory_gazette, bankruptcy_court, options_chain, global_pmi — the twelve
# entries in scripts/check_freshness.py's TIME_GATED set. Their upstream APIs
# serve a rolling recent window only: a live AIS position, a CT log tail, a DNS
# answer, a daily short-volume file that ages out. A day not collected is a day
# that cannot be bought back at any price. Weekday-only collection was silently
# discarding two of every seven days — ~28% — of the only data in this project
# that is not re-buyable. That data is the moat.
#
# Why this starts the WHOLE DAG instead of just the time-gated nodes:
# daily_collection cannot be invoked for a subset of its nodes. DAGExecutor.execute()
# (agent/pipeline/executor.py) takes only (dag, trigger); scripts/run_collection.py
# exposes --db-path/--workers/--dag and nothing finer; scripts/run_chain.py's
# --only selects whole DAGs, not nodes. The one node-level lever that exists,
# DAGNode.enabled (agent/pipeline/dag.py:39), is set programmatically by the
# ToolRoutingBandit inside build_daily_collection_dag() and no CLI reaches it.
# So the choice was "run the whole DAG at the weekend" or "pass a flag that does
# not exist". This runs the whole DAG.
#
# WHAT THE EXTRA WEEKEND RUN COSTS, measured rather than assumed. An earlier
# version of this comment claimed the weekday sources would dedup away and cost
# "network time, not rows and not DB growth". That is false for two of them:
#
#   - instrument_universe stamps observed_at with the RUN date, not the bar
#     date: `observed_at = time.mktime(as_of.timetuple())` with `as_of` defaulting
#     to `date.today()` (agent/tools/instrument_universe.py:879, :907), written
#     for instrument_return / instrument_volume / instrument_volatility. There is
#     no trading-day guard in the file. The dedup key is
#     (entity_id, source_tool, observation_type, observed_at, value_json)
#     (agent/pipeline/store.py:1469-1479), so a new observed_at is always a new
#     row — dedup cannot collapse it.
#   - options_chain has the same shape: persist_options_snapshot falls back to
#     time.time() (agent/tools/options_chain.py:129) and ingest_options_chains
#     passes no observed_at (:210), while summary["fetched_at"] is set to today's
#     date (:105), so value_json differs daily too.
#
# Read-only against the live DB, 2026-09-14..09-21: instrument_universe wrote 89
# rows on each weekday run and exactly 2 on each weekend day (the crypto pairs,
# which go through the bar-dated path and are genuinely weekend-traded). So a
# weekend run adds ~87 rows per day carrying FRIDAY's close stamped Saturday or
# Sunday, plus ~8-32 options_chain rows with the same defect. That is fabricated
# provenance, and a Layer 2 consumer reading instrument_return will see Friday's
# return repeated three times.
#
# It is still worth enabling, on an asymmetry: those fabricated rows are
# identifiable and deletable (instrument_universe rows whose observed_at falls
# on a Saturday or Sunday), while a missed AIS/CT/DNS window is gone at any
# price. Fix the provenance at the collector — pass the quote date into
# persist_options_snapshot, use the bar's own date instead of as_of=date.today()
# — and the cost goes to zero. Until then the cost is real and stated, not
# assumed away.
#
# CFTC is safe by contrast: agent/tools/cftc.py:640 derives observed_at from
# report_date, and its rows land only on the days COT actually publishes.
#
# Why Sat,Sun rather than OnCalendar=*-*-* 18:00 (a literal daily timer):
# the weekday slot is already collecting at 18:00 UTC, and a daily timer would
# fire a second collection run into the middle of the weekday run — two
# concurrent writers on .tirra_pipeline/pipeline.db, the single irreplaceable
# asset in this deployment. Sat,Sun plus the existing Mon..Fri gives one
# collection run per calendar day *in steady state*.
#
# "In steady state" is the honest limit of that claim, and NOTHING IN THIS REPO
# ENFORCES IT. grep for flock/LOCK_EX/Conflicts= across scripts/run_collection.py,
# scripts/run_chain.py, scripts/run_scheduled.sh and deploy/systemd/ returns
# nothing; PipelineStore's only write guard is a threading.RLock (store.py:486),
# in-process only, and store.py:1481-1486 says so outright — "does not make it
# atomic across processes; only a UNIQUE index will" — while the live table has
# no UNIQUE index. A second concurrent run also corrupts the executor's own
# zero-rows guard: DagRun captures domain_rows_before at executor.py:225 and
# diffs it afterwards (:133-140), so another run's inserts let a run that wrote
# nothing report rows landed. That is the exact green-but-empty signature this
# repo is trying to kill.
#
# Two follow-ups close it properly, and both live in files this timer does not
# own: wrap the collect/chain branches of scripts/run_scheduled.sh in
# `flock -n /opt/tirramind/.tirra_pipeline/.collect.lock`, and/or add
# `Conflicts=tirra-chain.service` to tirra-collect.service.
#
# NO Persistent= HERE, deliberately, and this is the one thing in this file that
# is a safety property rather than a preference. tirra-chain.timer carries
# Persistent=true and triggers a DIFFERENT unit (tirra-chain.service), so after
# downtime spanning a weekday slot and a weekend slot, systemd queues both
# catch-up jobs in the same boot transaction. systemd serialises starts of the
# same unit, not of two units — the collect-only deployment is safe only by
# accident, because tirra-collect.timer and this timer share Unit=. With
# Persistent=true here, a Monday boot after a Friday-through-Sunday outage would
# put two daily_collection processes into the live WAL database simultaneously,
# with no lock anywhere to stop them.
#
# The catch-up buys almost nothing to pay for that. The time-gated sources are
# point-in-time probes: a catch-up run on Monday fetches MONDAY's vessel
# position, CT log tail and DNS answer — the same window Monday's scheduled run
# fetches. It does not recover Saturday. Once the flock above exists, restore
# Persistent=true; until then the missed weekend run stays missed.
#
# Why 18:00 UTC, the same clock time as the weekday run: the time-gated sources
# are point-in-time probes. Sampling them at an even 24h spacing keeps the
# series comparable; an irregular interval resurfaces downstream as a spurious
# regime change.
#
# NOTE, do not repeat the earlier version's claim that this cadence "keeps
# COLLECTOR_SILENCE_DAYS=4 honest". It does the opposite. That constant's own
# docstring in scripts/check_freshness.py derives 4.0 from weekday-only
# collection — "a Friday run followed by a Monday run is 3 days apart; 4 allows
# one missed run on top of a weekend" — and the CADENCE_DAYS floors carry the
# same premise ("weekdays only, so any daily source must tolerate a weekend:
# 3 days minimum, never 1"). Under 7-day collection, 4 days of silence
# is four consecutive failed runs, where two would already be conclusive. The
# retune belongs in check_freshness.py, not here.
#
# Unit= points at the existing tirra-collect.service rather than duplicating its
# ExecStart, so "how collection runs" stays defined in exactly one file — and
# this timer works whether you enabled tirra-chain.timer or tirra-collect.timer
# for weekdays. On Sat/Sun only collection runs; no downstream DAG does. That is
# deliberate: the weekend job exists to capture perishable rows, and Monday's
# chain processes everything that accumulated.
#
# MEMORY: tirra-collect.service carries no MemoryMax/MemorySwapMax, while
# tirra-chain.service:37-41 caps itself at 1200M/1500M with a measured rationale
# (2026-08-27: 84% RSS, 1.24 GB swap on a 1 vCPU / 1.9 GB box; "a process
# OOM-killed mid-DAG leaves a run stuck in 'running' with no error recorded").
# A README-following deployment runs tirra-chain on weekdays, so
# tirra-collect.service never ran before this timer existed and now runs twice a
# week uncapped. Copy that pair onto tirra-collect.service — daily_collection is
# a strict subset of the chain's workload, so the same ceiling is headroom, not
# a constraint. That edit belongs to tirra-collect.service.
OnCalendar=Sat,Sun 18:00 UTC
Unit=tirra-collect.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
